      },
      "tests": [
        {
          "id": "scm_surface_noon",
          "description": "Every CBM-Z rate coefficient at the surface layer of the idealized chem_opt = 170 column at local noon, step 364 level 1 (T = 309.11 K, p = 996.3 hPa, M = 2.3344e19 molec/cm3, j(NO2) = 5.56e-3 /s), against RCONST(1:142) written by the fork's chem/KPP/inc/cbmz_mosaic/kpp_mechd_*_cbmz_mosaic.inc hooks straight after Update_Rconst. This is the warm, fully photolysing, peroxy-rich regime: the boundary-layer radical pool is at its largest here, so the ten lumped peroxy-peroxy coefficients k_R127..k_R136 are exercised at their biggest values.\n\nReference and tolerance. The values are RCONST as WRF-Chem itself computed it, cross-checked bit for bit (Linf = 0 at all 16 dumped steps and all 59 levels) by the standalone real64 replay kernels/cbmz_driver, which calls cbmz_mosaic_Update_RCONST on the dumped TEMP, C_M, C_H2O and jv. WRF is built single precision, but the KPP interface promotes t_phy, rho_phy and the photolysis rates to KIND = dp = real64 before Update_Rconst, so every rate law is evaluated in double there; the dumped inputs are bound here as their exact binary64 values, hence rel 1e-9. Of the 568 assertions across the four regimes 529 are BIT-IDENTICAL to WRF's KPP and the largest residual anywhere is 8.5e-16 (k_R127, surface noon); in this regime it is 8.5e-16 (k_R127). Every one of the 39 inexact assertions is a peroxy-pool coefficient or a Troe/JPL form, i.e. the two places where this document evaluates the same algebra in a different order from the Fortran, and all of them are within four ulp. Dump: data/eqwefic/dumps/chem170_wrf/esm_dump_cbmz_kpp_364.json, level k = 1; the run is described in data/eqwefic/notes/chem170_scm_reference_run.md.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0,
            "end": 1
          },
          "tolerance": {
            "rel": 1e-09
          },
          "parameter_overrides": {
            "T": 309.1141357421875,
